  expressible) → [1mchange events[0m (something to notify [3mabout[0m) → [1mnotifications[0m (subscription + delivery + read state).
  Notifications is last because every earlier piece constrains it.

  Before I propose that split concretely, the fork that reshapes all of it:

  [1mWhat differs:[0m whether there is more than one person in the system. Notifications for [3mother[0m people require accounts, a
  server, sessions, and a delivery channel that works while you're not looking at the page. Notifications for [3myourself[0m
  are a UI affordance over local state and need none of that.

  - [1mMulti-user, server-backed[0m — buys real "users get notified"; costs a backend, auth, a database, and a deploy story.
    Hard to add later if the data model starts local-only, so this is the expensive one to defer.
  - [1mSingle-user, browser-only[0m — buys a working app fast with no infrastructure; costs the ability to notify anyone but
    yourself. "Tasks I care about" collapses to flags on your own tasks.
  - [1mSingle-user now, multi-user shaped[0m — buys speed while keeping IDs and ownership fields in the model so a server can
    slot in later; costs some upfront care and it's still a real migration, not free.

  [1mMy recommendation:[0m the third, [3mif[0m multi-user is genuinely coming. Designing the task model with owner and actor fields
  from day one costs almost nothing now and is the part that's painful to retrofit. If multi-user isn't actually the
  goal, the second — don't build auth for an audience of one.
